block potential sequences. Also, keeping W6 allows for the longer W4-5-6-7-8 sequence which can be split into two chows. \n\nTherefore, the best action is to play T3.\n</think>\n\nEvaluation: Playing T3 allows maintaining potential chows in the W4-W8 sequence and avoids blocking future melds. Discarding T3 is safe as it's a terminal tile not part of any immediate chow.\nReason: T3 is a terminal tile not contributing to current sequences. Keeping W4-W8 sequence intact provides flexibility for forming two chows (4-5-6 and 7-8-9 if T9 is paired). Discarding T3 maintains concealed hand status and avoids giving others key tiles for their melds.\nAnswer: Play T3"}
