rt of any useful sequence and allows focusing on the Character suit sequences for higher scoring patterns like Pure Straight or All Chows.\n</think>\n\nEvaluation: The hand contains a strong sequence in Characters (W4-W9) and multiple T5s. Discarding T3, which is isolated and not part of a sequence, preserves the potential for high-scoring Pure Straight or All Chows patterns. Retaining W4-W9 allows for multiple Chows in one suit.  \nReason: T3 is a non-essential tile in Dots with no sequence potential, while the Characters sequence (W4-W9) can form multiple Chows. Discarding T3 minimizes disruption to the high-value Character suit sequences.  \nAnswer: Play T3."}
